{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01SGS4Cv1oLVAfmR7m166nz4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# libsodium → Rust translation conventions (READ FULLY BEFORE WRITING CODE)\n2\t\n3\tWorkspace root: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-tUprtH` (called `$W` below).\n4\t\n5\t* `$W/c_src/` — the original C. **READ ONLY. NEVER MODIFY.**\n6\t* `$W/translation/` — the Rust crate (`cdylib`, no external dependencies, edition 2021).\n7\t* `$W/tools/cpp.sh <file.c>` — prints the *post-preprocessor* libsodium source for a\n8\t  `.c` file, with all `#ifdef HAVE_*` already resolved and system-header noise stripped.\n9\t  **Use this** — it removes all guesswork about which branch is compiled.\n10\t\n11\t## Build configuration being reproduced\n12\t\n13\tThe reference CMake build compiles every `.c` under `c_src/libsodium/` with **no\n14\t`HAVE_*` macros defined at all** (equivalent to `configure --disable-asm`).\n15\tConsequences you must honour:\n16\t\n17\t* `HAVE_TI_MODE` is **NOT** defined → `fe25519` is `int32_t[10]` (fe_25_5), poly1305 uses\n18\t  the 32-bit donna code, etc.\n19\t* `NATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN` / `NATIVE_BIG_ENDIAN` **NOT** defined → byte-wise load/store.\n20\t* No SIMD (`HAVE_*INTRIN_H`, `HAVE_ARMCRYPTO`, `HAVE_AVX*`, …) → only the soft/ref\n21\t  implementations exist; `pick_best_implementation()` always selects them.\n22\t* `HAVE_PTHREAD` / `HAVE_ATOMIC_OPS` **NOT** defined → `sodium_crit_enter/leave` are no-ops.\n23\t* Compiler-predefined macros ARE defined: `__GNUC__`, `__ELF__`, `__x86_64__`,\n24\t  `__linux__`, `__SIZEOF_INT128__`, `__LITTLE_ENDIAN__`-equivalents, etc.\n25\t* `CONFIGURED` is not defined, `DEV_MODE` is not defined (only affects `#warning`s).\n26\t\n27\tIf in doubt, run `$W/tools/cpp.sh` on the file. That is the ground truth.\n28\t\n29\t## Crate layout\n30\t\n31\t`src/lib.rs` already declares every module — **do not edit `lib.rs`**.\n32\tShared helper modules you should use:\n33\t\n34\t* `crate::common` — `load32_le/store32_le/load64_le/store64_le/load32_be/store32_be/\n35\t  load64_be/store64_be/rotl32/rotl64/rotr32/rotr64/xor_buf/SODIUM_SIZE_MAX`.\n36\t  Signatures use raw pointers, e.g. `unsafe fn load64_le(src: *const u8) -> u64`.\n37\t* `crate::csys` — hand-rolled libc bindings (`malloc`, `free`, `memcpy`, `memset`,\n38\t  `memcmp`, `strlen`, `sysconf`, `mmap`, `mprotect`, `abort`, `errno()`, `set_errno()`,\n39\t  errno/prot/open constants, …). Add more `extern \"C\"` declarations locally in your own\n40\t  module if you need something that is missing — do NOT edit `csys.rs`\n41\t  (duplicate `extern \"C\"` declarations across modules are fine).\n42\t* `crate::types` — `#[repr(C)]` types that cross module boundaries: `fe25519`,\n43\t  `ge25519_p2/p3/p1p1/precomp/cached`, `crypto_hash_sha256_state`,\n44\t  `crypto_hash_sha512_state`, `crypto_generichash_blake2b_state`, `blake2b_state`,\n45\t  `randombytes_implementation`.\n46\t\n47\t## Rules for every exported function\n48\t\n49\t1. Signature: `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn NAME(...) -> ...`\n50\t   (this crate uses edition 2021; write `#[no_mangle]` — the `unsafe(...)` form is\n51\t   not needed. Use exactly `#[no_mangle]`.)\n52\t2. **The `NAME` must be the final linker symbol.** `include/sodium/private/quirks.h`\n53\t   `#define`s many internal names to a `_sodium_`-prefixed symbol, e.g.\n54\t   `blake2b_init` → `_sodium_blake2b_init`, `ge25519_p3_add` → `_sodium_ge25519_p3_add`,\n55\t   `argon2_ctx` → `_sodium_argon2_ctx`. Every non-`static` function in a file that\n56\t   includes `private/quirks.h` (directly or transitively — almost all do) must be\n57\t   checked against that list. `$W/tools/cpp.sh` output shows the renamed name directly.\n58\t3. Use `core::ffi` types (`c_int`, `c_char`, `c_void`, `c_ulonglong`, …) or the exact\n59\t   Rust equivalent (`unsigned long long` → `u64`, `size_t` → `usize`,\n60\t   `unsigned char *` → `*mut u8`, `const unsigned char *` → `*const u8`).\n61\t4. `static` C functions become private Rust `unsafe fn` (no `#[no_mangle]`).\n62\t5. Exported **data** objects (e.g. `crypto_stream_chacha20_ref_implementation`,\n63\t   `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation`, `aegis128l_soft_implementation`) must be\n64\t   `#[no_mangle] pub static NAME: Type = ...;` with a `#[repr(C)]` type. If the C\n65\t   object is non-`const` and mutated, use `pub static mut`.\n66\t6. Reproduce behaviour **exactly**, including bugs, error-check order, integer\n67\t   overflow/wrapping, and undefined-ish behaviour. Use `wrapping_*`,\n68\t   `unchecked` casts (`as`), and raw pointers freely. The crate is compiled with\n69\t   `overflow-checks = false`, but still prefer `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_mul` so the\n70\t   result is unambiguous.\n71\t7. C `int` shifts/promotions: be careful to mirror C integer promotion. When a C\n72\t   expression mixes `unsigned char` and `int`, do the arithmetic in `i32`/`u32` as C\n73\t   would, then truncate.\n74\t8. Never `panic!`. No bounds-checked indexing on data whose length you cannot prove;\n75\t   use `*ptr.add(i)` for pointer walks. Local fixed arrays may be indexed normally.\n76\t9. `sodium_misuse()` (declared in `core.h`) is `extern \"C\" fn sodium_misuse() -> !`.\n77\t   Declare it locally as `fn sodium_misuse();` in an `extern \"C\"` block if you need it.\n78\t10. `COMPILER_ASSERT(X)` is a compile-time assert; either drop it or use\n79\t    `const _: () = assert!(...);`.\n80\t\n81\t## Cross-module calls\n82\t\n83\tModules are decoupled: to call a function that lives in another Rust module, declare it\n84\tin a local `extern \"C\" { ... }` block using the **final linker name** and the exact C\n85\tsignature from the header. Do not try to `use` it from the other module. Example:\n86\t\n87\t```rust\n88\textern \"C\" {\n89\t    fn crypto_hash_sha512(out: *mut u8, inp: *const u8, inlen: u64) -> c_int;\n90\t    #[link_name = \"_sodium_ge25519_p3_add\"]\n91\t    fn ge25519_p3_add(r: *mut ge25519_p3, p: *const ge25519_p3, q: *const ge25519_p3);\n92\t}\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\t(The cdylib links with undefined symbols allowed while other modules are still stubs.)\n96\t\n97\t## Verifying your work\n98\t\n99\t```bash\n100\tcd $W/translation && cargo build --release --target-dir /tmp-unique-dir\n101\t```\n102\tUse a **unique** `--target-dir` (e.g. `$W/_chk/<yourname>`) so parallel agents do not\n103\tfight over the lock. `cargo build` on a cdylib tolerates undefined symbols, so a\n104\tsuccessful build means your module type-checks.\n105\t\n106\t**Only edit the file(s) you were assigned.** If `cargo build` reports errors in other\n107\tmodules, ignore them (they belong to other agents) — but make sure there are ZERO\n108\terrors attributed to your own file(s).\n109\t\n110\tReference symbol lists: `$W/_cbuild/csyms.txt` (all 890 exports of the C `.so`) and\n111\t`$W/_cbuild/persym.txt` (exports grouped per object file). After finishing, check that\n112\tevery symbol listed for your `.c` files appears in your `.rs` file.\n113\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01WbjBggSPdj2SuTgwsRafmw","session_id":"3f891fde-b339-4353-9d37-1cfde3da52f8","uuid":"7cbb4e65-6e9a-4200-9863-6aabb76c4ecf","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:11:39.340Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate KEM frontends"}
